window.docContents[23].resolve({"/Encapsulation/Inheritance/#Core-C______--Encapsulation--Inheritance":{"contents":"A pointer to a derived class is accepted where a pointer to its base is expected, in declarations, assignments, arguments, returns, comparisons and the branches of ?:. It is the only conversion between pointers to classes, and the other direction is a type error.\n\nA method is virtual when it is declared virtual or marked override, Method.virtual. An override is virtual without the word virtual, as in C++ (N4659 §13.3 paragraph 2). A third class of a chain may therefore override it again, and a call through a pointer to the middle class dispatches.The class tag  of the receiver chooses a virtual method, the nearest declaration in the chain of , so a call through a base pointer reaches the override of the derived class. The static class  chooses a non virtual method.A derived class redefines only a method that is virtual in the base, and marks it override. So the method reached from  and the one reached from  coincide for every non virtual method.Redefining a non virtual method, marking override without a virtual method in a base, or changing the signature is a type error.\n\nFor every well typed program, the method a call resolves to by the static class of the receiver and the method it resolves to by the class tag coincide whenever the method is not virtual.The nearest declaration of a non virtual  from  is also the nearest from , because no class between  and  redeclares  with its signature. The property follows from the rule T-Class by inspection, and there is no Lean proof.\n\nThe command delete e applies to a pointer to a class or to an object type of the library, whose rule DeleteLib in  frees what the module owns and then the location of the instance.On an object it runs the destructors of the chain from the tag up to the root, each with this bound to the object, and removes the record and the field locations from .A second delete and any later access through the pointer have no derivation. So has a delete through a pointer to a base without a virtual destructor in its chain. The evaluator gives error in the three cases, which C++17 leaves undefined (N4659 §8.3.5 paragraphs 2 and 3, §6.7 paragraph 4). The command delete nullptr runs no destructor, as in C++ (N4659 §8.3.5 paragraph 6).\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Encapsulation","header":"6.3. Inheritance","id":"/Encapsulation/Inheritance/#Core-C______--Encapsulation--Inheritance"}});